---
/* THE DETAIL — the layer the brief asked for and no round ever built.

   refs/BRIEF.md, site map: "Pilots — the four 2026 pilots only. Cards
   first. Detail on click or a short dedicated block." The one-line-per-
   field cap governs the CARD. This is the short dedicated block.

   ── THE COPY RULE, AND THE TWO CLAUSES IT SETTLES ───────────────────

   The brief says both "at most 3 short lines of supporting text" for any
   section AND "detail on click or a short dedicated block" for this one.
   A block that answers what it is, who it is for, where it would run, how
   it is measured and what it still needs cannot do it in three lines, so
   the two clauses have to be read together rather than one over the other.
   They are read like this, and the block is built to the reading:

     PROSE is capped at THREE SHORT LINES. One tagline (one line, all four)
     and one outcome (two lines, all four). Nothing else in the block is a
     sentence a reader has to follow.

     FACTS are labelled and capped at ONE SHORT LINE EACH — the same cap
     the brief puts on a card field, applied to the six fields here. A
     labelled one-line fact is not supporting text; it is the row of a
     dossier, and the brief's own pilot-card rule is the precedent.

   That is the whole budget, and it is measured, not asserted: 9 lines a
   block at 1440 (3 of prose, 6 one-line facts) and 42-48 words, 178 across
   the layer — down from 13-14 lines and 62-74 words a block, 269 across.
   The old count was a breach of the cap under any reading of it. Any string added here
   is a string taken out. If a fact needs a second line to be true, the
   fact is wrong for this block, not the line.

   It is written for one reader: a programme officer who has to decide
   whether to fund one of these. She needs, in this order —

     WHAT IT IS      one sentence of what the thing actually does
     WHO IT IS FOR   the population, named
     WHERE           the kind of place it would run, in the conditional
     MEASURED BY     the one outcome, which is also the card's GOAL
     WHAT IT NEEDS   money, a technology, somewhere to run it

   …and above all, THE ASK. refs/PROSPECTUS.txt calls these FOUR ACTIVE
   PILOTS and says the Institute is working with three of the four named
   partners, so the right-hand plate can no longer be headed "What it still
   needs" — that asserted a deficiency in an active pilot, which no source
   carries. It is headed "Where a partner comes in", which is what the
   prospectus's own model describes: the Institute matches each pilot with a
   technology partner and recruits implementation partners, and a Core
   Partner's dedicated pilot funding is "deployed across pilot opportunities
   the partner selects to support". The plate is still as long as the left
   column, and the shape is still the ask: this much is designed, this is
   the seat that is open.

   THE PHOTOGRAPH IS A PLATE, NOT A SCREEN. One column wide — about 530px
   at 1440, a third of the width and a third of the height the old card
   spent — hung at the top of the right column beside the words. Nothing is
   set on any frame except its corner credit.

   WHY NOT A FULL-BLEED BAND, which was the first fix tried: a full-bleed
   strip is still 1440px of image, and cover-cropping a 3:2 portrait into
   5:1 at that width turns a woman holding her infant into a two-hundred-
   pixel face under the words INFANT MORTALITY. Shortening a full-bleed
   frame reduces how much of the screen a person occupies and INCREASES how
   large they are rendered. Only narrowing the frame does both. The
   photographs are now used at a scale where a person in one of them is a
   person in a room. See refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md.

   ── ROUND 9. TWO THINGS WERE MERELY FINE ────────────────────────────

   1. THE HELD SPACE WAS A RESIDUAL, NOT AN INTERVAL. The header row's
      height is set by the photograph (374px at 1440). The left column
      carried 98px of title-and-tagline and a 59–94px outcome, and
      `justify-content: space-between` dumped whatever was left — 182px on
      pilot 01, 217px on pilot 03 — into one unmarked gap in the middle of
      a column. A pause whose size is decided by how many lines the outcome
      happens to wrap to is not a pause; it is slack, and it measured
      35px different from one block to the next in a set of four that a
      reader scrolls through in ten seconds.

      The interval is now DECLARED (`--pd-hold`, the middle track of an
      explicit three-row grid) and the two blocks that bound it are set to
      a fixed line count on all four pilots: the tagline to a measure that
      breaks every one of them to two lines, the outcome likewise. (Round
      10 kept the fixed line count and inverted the tagline's half of it:
      one line on all four, which is both shorter and sturdier than a
      forced wrap. The header rows still measure identically — hold 152px,
      row 358px, on all four at 1440.) Every
      header row in the set is now metrically identical, and the space
      between the name and the outcome is the same held beat four times
      running. It also shrank, because the type either side grew into it:
      the outcome moved up a rung to `.bridge--lift`, which is the size the
      one fact a funder is asked to believe should have been all along.

   2. THE TWO COLUMNS BELOW THE RULE DID NOT MEET. The left head sat at
      y=2370 and "Where a partner comes in" at y=2403 — 33px apart, because the
      right-hand head was INSIDE the plate and wearing its padding. Worse,
      the left column's FIRST DATA ROW landed on exactly the right column's
      HEAD line, which asserts a relationship that is false. And the two
      lists were built differently — term-beside-value on the left, term-
      over-value on the right — so nothing could be read across.

      The head is now out of the plate and set as a peer of "The pilot"
      on one line; the plate's top EDGE lands on the left column's first
      row; and the open list is the same object as the pilot list —
      same rail, same row pitch, same two columns — on a lighter ground.
      That is the argument the block exists to make, and it now reads in
      one glance: the same shape twice, one filled in on the page, one
      still open on a plate.

   ── ROUND 10. THE COPY, AND THE ONE THING NOT DRAWN FOUR TIMES ──────

   1. THE BUDGET WAS BREACHED. See the copy rule above. Each block carried
      13 to 14 lines of supporting text and 62 to 74 words; the layer
      carried 269. Three cuts, no new objects: `Proposed by` left the
      dossier (it set the partner's name 60px under the same partner's
      name on the rail), the tagline came down to one line on all four,
      and every one of the six remaining values was rewritten as a single
      short line. `who` now names the PEOPLE and `where` the PLACE, which
      is what stopped pilot 03 saying "Indianapolis" twice in three rows.

   2. THE RAIL WAS THE ONE THING NOT DRAWN FOUR TIMES. See the note on
      the 700px query below. `margin-left: auto` held the field word to
      the right edge on a line that was only sometimes full: at 390 it
      fitted on pilots 01 and 03 and broke on 02 and 04, leaving a
      right-aligned ENERGY / EDUCATION alone above the title with nothing
      to its left. Rail heights measured 12 / 35 / 12 / 35. The break is
      now declared, not discovered: 36 / 36 / 36 / 36 at 390, at 360 and
      at 320, and the two lines say the same two things every time.

   3. AND THE DOSSIER TURNS AT 1280 rather than wrapping a one-line fact
      to three inside the plate. See that query.

   ── ROUND 11. THE PICTURE WAS DRIVING AND THE WORDS WERE PASSENGERS ──

   This supersedes round 9's two items above. Both of them solved the
   wrong problem well: they made the record's TWO ROWS internally tidy
   while leaving the record's height set, twice over, by the right-hand
   column. Measured at 1440 before this round —

     /pilots/  5808px tall, the tallest page on the site, taller than the
               homepage, to carry the same four facts /partner/ carries in
               about 530px. 7843px at 390.
     the left column held 1184px of dead vertical space in gaps over
     150px (/partner/, the site at its best, holds 721):
       253  record 01's last line  →  record 02's number
       230  record 03's last line  →  record 04's number
       206  record 02's last line  →  record 03's number
       174  the layer's own bridge →  record 01's number
       169  the turn's eyebrow     →  its sentence
       152  record 02's tagline    →  its own MEASURED BY

   Every one of those is the same defect. The header row was `auto /
   1fr / auto` with the photograph spanning all three, so the 1fr track
   was whatever the picture's aspect ratio left over — 107px on three
   records and 152px on the one whose outcome wrapped shorter. Below it
   the second grid did it again: a 158px dossier in the left column
   against a 283px plate in the right, so the words stopped and the
   record kept going for another 125px. Twice per record, four times
   over. Whitespace everywhere else on this site is an interval the eye
   crosses on purpose; here it was a by-product of an image's aspect
   ratio.

   THE FIX IS NOT A CUT. Every fact in the record is sourced and
   load-bearing and /pilots/ is the expanded view — it is the page a
   programme officer reads once she has picked a favourite. What changed
   is where the objects sit:

     ONE grid, not two. The words run down the left column in a single
     continuous column — name, dossier, outcome — on ONE declared
     interval used twice. No track anywhere in the record is sized by
     the photograph, and no gap in the left column exceeds that interval.

     THE THREE OPEN NEEDS CAME OUT OF THE RIGHT COLUMN. They were a
     538px-wide stack 257px tall and they were the whole of the second
     imbalance. They are three one-line facts and they are peers, so
     they are set across the foot of the record as a row of three with
     the door at the end of it — which is also the shape /partner/'s
     table is praised for. The plate, the ground and the brass wipe on
     the door are unchanged; only the axis is.

   Round 9's claim that "the same shape twice" was the argument the block
   exists to make does not survive the measurement. The two lists are not
   the same shape: one is a four-row dossier read down, the other is
   three peers read across. Drawing them alike cost the record 150px of
   dead column and bought a rhyme nobody could see, because the two lists
   were never on screen together in one glance.

   After: no gap over 150px anywhere in the left column of /pilots/, and
   the record's own intervals are identical on all four.
*/
import Figure from './Figure.astro';

const { pilot: p, loading = 'lazy', focal = '50% 50%', focalSm, grade = 0.5 } = Astro.props;
const d = p.detail;

/* ── ONE GRADE, FOUR FRAMES ──────────────────────────────────────────
   The four plates were four unrelated pictures in four identical frames:
   measured over the exact 537.7 x 358.4 box at 1440, mean chroma ran
   41.7 / 22.0 / 20.7 / 13.3 and the hue swung warm-red -> cool-blue ->
   warm -> neutral. A frame is not a grade; four frames that match while
   the pictures inside them do not is the thing that makes a set read as
   stock. The trim per source lives with the photograph in site.js
   (`pilot.plate`) because it is a fact about that FILE, not about this
   layout — and it wins over anything the page passes, since a page-level
   crop or grade guess cannot know what the meter said. */
const pl = p.plate ?? {};
const hid = `${p.id}-h`;

/* THREE AND THREE. `Proposed by` is gone: it set the partner's name in
   a dossier row 60px under the same partner's name on the rail. What is
   left is three facts against three gaps — the shape the block exists to
   show — and every value is one short line (see the copy rule above). */
const proposal = [
  { t: 'What it does', v: d.what },
  { t: 'Who it is for', v: d.who },
  /* Conditional for 04 only. The prospectus calls 01-03 active and says
     it 'is exploring' 04, so a single tense here is wrong for one of the
     four blocks whichever tense is chosen. The status drives it. */
  { t: p.status === 'Exploring' ? 'Where it would run' : 'Where it runs', v: d.where },
];

const needs = [
  { t: 'Funding', v: d.needs.fund },
  { t: 'Technology', v: d.needs.build },
  { t: 'Somewhere to run it', v: d.needs.run },
];
---
<section class="pd" id={p.id} aria-labelledby={hid}>
  <div class="shell pd__in">
    <p class="pd__rail" data-reveal>
      <span class="index pd__i">{p.index}</span>
      <span class="eyebrow pd__partner">{p.partner}</span>
      {/* the rail's break, declared. Inert above 700px. */}
      <span class="pd__brk" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="label pd__q">{p.status}</span>
      <span class="label pd__field">{p.field}</span>
    </p>

    {/* THE RECORD, AS ONE TWO-COLUMN COMPOSITION.

         Left: the words, top to bottom, in one continuous run — the name,
         what is designed, what it is measured by. Right: the photograph.
         Below both, full width: what is still open, and the door.

         The words are no longer distributed against the picture's height.
         They are set to their own rhythm and the picture is hung at the
         top of the column beside them. See the round-11 note above. */}
    <div class="pd__body">
      <div class="pd__say">
        <div class="pd__say-top">
          <h2 class="display display--head pd__t lines" id={hid}>{p.title}</h2>
          <p class="pd__tag" data-reveal="120">{p.tagline}</p>
        </div>

        {/* what is designed */}
        <div class="pd__group" data-reveal="140">
          <p class="label label--brass pd__gl">The pilot</p>
          <dl class="pd__dl" data-seq="90" data-seq-from="40">
            {proposal.map((r) => (
              <div data-reveal><dt class="label">{r.t}</dt><dd>{r.v}</dd></div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>

        {/* THE OUTCOME. The single fact a funder is being asked to
             believe, and the one the Institute promises to measure and
             share — so it is set at the rung above the tagline and it
             closes the column. */}
        <div class="pd__goal" data-reveal="180">
          <p class="label label--brass">Measured by</p>
          <p class="bridge bridge--lift bridge--fg pd__goal-t">{p.goal}</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pd__plate" data-reveal="60">
        <Figure
          src={p.image}
          alt={p.alt ?? ''}
          ratio="3 / 2"
          ratioSm="4 / 3"
          grade={pl.grade ?? grade}
          sat={pl.sat ?? 1}
          lift={pl.lift ?? 1}
          tint={pl.tint ?? 1}
          warm={pl.warm ?? 0}
          zoom={pl.zoom ?? 1}
          zoomSm={pl.zoomSm}
          focal={pl.focal ?? focal}
          focalSm={pl.focalSm ?? focalSm ?? pl.focal ?? focal}
          parallax={3}
          loading={loading}
          credit={p.credit}
          creditPos="bottom-right"
          {/* The only ink any of these frames needs is under its own credit,
              and the credit is in the bottom RIGHT corner — so the ramp is
              flipped and kept short. `auto` left that corner at L* 47 on two
              of the four, which is twice the ceiling the micro register can
              sit on (tokens.css, "legibility budget"). */}
          scrim="bottom"
          align="right"
          reach={0.55}
          rise={0.38}
          ink={1}
          vig={0.35}
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* ── and what is not ──────────────────────────────────────────
         The three open needs were a stacked list 538px wide in the right
         column, 257px tall, and they were the reason the record's right
         side ran a hundred and fifty pixels past its left. They are three
         one-line facts and they are peers, so across the foot of the
         record is where they belong: one glance, one row, and the door at
         the end of it. */}
    <div class="pd__foot">
      <p class="label label--brass pd__gl" data-reveal="60">Where a partner comes in</p>
      <div class="pd__open on-plate" data-reveal="80">
        <dl class="pd__dl pd__dl--open">
          {needs.map((r) => (
            <div><dt class="label">{r.t}</dt><dd>{r.v}</dd></div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <a class="pd__ask" href="/partner/">
          <span class="pd__ask-t">Partner on this pilot</span>
          <svg class="arrow pd__arrow" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M16 1l5 4-5 4M21 5H0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" fill="none"/>
          </svg>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .pd {
    position: relative;
    overflow-x: clip;
    /* the anchor from the card layer must never land a title under the bar */
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 2.5rem);

    /* ── the block's two constants, stated once ──────────────────────
       --pd-rail  the label column. Every term in the record hangs off it,
                  so the reader has ONE left edge for names and ONE for
                  values, top to bottom.
       --pd-inset the open plate's padding. */
    --pd-rail: 10.5rem;
    --pd-inset: clamp(1.35rem, 1.9vw, 1.5rem);
    /* the shared right edge every object takes once the block is stacked */
    --pd-stack: 34rem;
  }
  /* The record now ENDS on a bordered plate rather than on a loose last
     line, and a hard terminator needs less air after it than a soft one.
     The separation came down accordingly: 86px + the block's own 42px of
     lead measured 156 from the last value of one record to the number of
     the next, which was the only interval left in the left column over
     150. It is 141 now, and it is the same on all three joins. */
  .pd + .pd { margin-top: clamp(2.6rem, 5vw, 5.5rem); }

  .pd__in { padding-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.6rem); }

  .pd__rail {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 1.1ch; flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  /* The separator is optically centred, not mechanically: the label register
     is tracked out 0.12em, so the word before the dot already carries a
     sliver of trailing air the word after it does not. The gap in front is
     the flex gap less that sliver. */
  .pd__q { color: var(--fg-meta); white-space: nowrap; }
  .pd__q::before { content: '\00b7'; margin-right: 0.95ch; margin-left: -0.12ch; }
  .pd__field { margin-left: auto; white-space: nowrap; }
  .pd__brk { display: none; }

  /* ── THE BODY: words left, picture right ─────────────────────────
     One grid, one pair of columns, one row. The left column is a
     continuous run of type with ONE interval, used twice; the right
     column is the photograph, hung from the top so its edge lands on the
     title's. Nothing in the left column is sized by the picture and
     nothing in the picture is sized by the words. */
  .pd__body {
    margin-top: clamp(0.85rem, 1.6vw, 1.3rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.38fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* THE INTERVAL. One value, twice: name → dossier → outcome. It is a
     declared beat, identical on all four records and independent of how
     many lines any block happens to wrap to. */
  .pd__say {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    row-gap: clamp(1.7rem, 3.3vw, 2.9rem);
  }
  .pd__say-top { display: grid; gap: clamp(0.7rem, 1.4vw, 1.1rem); }
  .pd__goal { display: grid; gap: clamp(0.55rem, 1.1vw, 0.85rem); }
  .pd__plate { align-self: start; position: relative; }

  .pd__t { margin: 0; line-height: 1.0; letter-spacing: -0.02em; text-wrap: initial; }
  /* ONE LINE, on all four. A measure wide enough for the longest of the
     four taglines (52 characters) sets every one of them to one line,
     which is both the shorter reading and the sturdier one — a one-line
     block cannot wrap differently from its neighbours. */
  .pd__tag {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: var(--step-lede);
    line-height: 1.45;
    max-width: 54ch;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  /* TWO LINES, on all four — which is what the copy rule at the head of
     this file says the outcome is, and what 20ch stopped delivering the
     moment the outcome moved out of a narrow slot and into the full left
     column: three of the four ran to three lines there. The measure is
     set to the longest of the four (60 characters), so every one of them
     breaks once and none of them breaks twice. */
  .pd__goal-t { margin: 0; max-width: 32ch; }

  /* THE GROUP HEADS. The space under a brass head has to be clearly
     larger than the space inside the field it heads, or "The pilot" reads
     as a fourth term rather than the name of the three below it. */
  .pd__gl { margin: 0 0 clamp(1rem, 1.7vw, 1.5rem); }

  /* a dossier: term left, value right, on one baseline */
  .pd__dl { margin: 0; display: grid; row-gap: clamp(0.75rem, 1.5vw, 1.15rem); }
  .pd__dl > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--pd-rail)) minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.75rem);
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .pd__dl dt { color: var(--fg-meta); }
  .pd__dl dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 46ch;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ── the foot: what is still open, across the record ────────────── */
  .pd__foot {
    margin-top: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 3rem);
    padding-top: clamp(1.3rem, 2.4vw, 2rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .pd__open {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    column-gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
    align-items: end;
    /* less its own 1px border on the top edge, so the register inside the
       plate sits at the same inset as the rules above it */
    padding: calc(var(--pd-inset) - 1px) clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 2rem) clamp(1.2rem, 2.2vw, 1.7rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
  }
  /* THREE PEERS, READ ACROSS. Term over value, because the three values
     are short and a row of three one-line facts is a row, not a list. */
  .pd__dl--open {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
    row-gap: 1.15rem;
  }
  .pd__dl--open > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); row-gap: 0.3rem; }
  .pd__dl--open dd { max-width: 30ch; }

  .pd__ask {
    position: relative;
    align-self: end;
    padding-top: clamp(0.9rem, 1.8vw, 1.3rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.9ch;
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: var(--step-body);
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* the same gesture the card layer uses: a brass hairline wipes along the
     rule the door already sits on, so the two pilot layers rhyme */
  .pd__ask::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; top: -1px; left: 0; right: 0; height: 1px;
    background: var(--brass);
    transform: scaleX(0); transform-origin: right;
    transition: transform 620ms var(--ease-out);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .pd__arrow {
    width: 22px; color: var(--brass);
    opacity: 0.62; transform: translateX(-4px);
    transition: opacity var(--dur-hover) var(--ease-out), transform 520ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .pd__ask:hover::before { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }
    .pd__ask:hover .pd__arrow { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0.3rem); }
  }
  /* The house ring, unmodified — base.css's 3px, which is the value
     written for a word and an arrow on a rule. */
  .pd__ask:focus-visible::before { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }
  .pd__ask:focus-visible .pd__arrow { opacity: 1; transform: none; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pd__arrow, .pd__ask::before { transition-duration: 1ms; }
    /* The ask lights, the arrow does not slide. */
    .pd__ask:hover .pd__arrow, .pd__ask:focus-visible .pd__arrow { transform: none; }
  }

  /* ── THE FOOT TURNS IN TWO STAGES ───────────────────────────────
     Three tracks AND the door need about 1100px of plate. Below that the
     door drops to its own line under the three — which is where it was
     before this round, and it keeps its rule and its brass wipe. The
     three needs themselves hold their row much further down, and only
     give it up at 1000, where the record has already gone to one column
     and the plate is capped to the stacked width. */
  @media (max-width: 1150px) {
    .pd__open { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pd__ask {
      margin-top: clamp(1.2rem, 2.2vw, 1.7rem);
      justify-self: start;
    }
  }
  /* ── Narrow ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .pd__dl--open { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

    /* one column, and the photograph comes BETWEEN the tagline and the
       dossier: name the thing, show the thing, then say what it is. The
       left column releases its own grid so the plate can take its place
       in the run. */
    .pd__body { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); row-gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.4rem); }
    .pd__say { display: contents; }
    .pd__say-top { order: 1; }
    .pd__plate  { order: 2; }
    .pd__group  { order: 3; }
    .pd__goal   { order: 4; }
    .pd__tag { max-width: 38ch; }
    .pd__goal-t { max-width: 26ch; }

    /* ONE COLUMN MEANS ONE COLUMN. Stacked, the photograph, the lists and
